finding: 1. src/standard/rendering/cluster.ts:545 and src/standard/rendering/shadows.ts:631 still use view.width/view.height, whereas src/core/rendering/substrate.ts:202 now projects using view.projectionAspect. A rectangle plus a Resolution with a different target aspect (or ordinary fractional backing-size rounding) therefore builds light-cluster bounds and shadow fits for a different frustum than the camera draws. Independently exercised sizeCameraViewport with CSS target 64x48, backing/Resolution 32x32, rectangle (40,4,16,32): scene 8x21, actual projection aspect 0.5, cluster/shadow aspect 0.380952; cluster half-width 0.219943 versus correct 0.288675. This can omit lights and shadow coverage in visible edge regions. The added projection test uses exact integer sizes and no lights/shadows/Resolution, so all new tests pass despite it. Propagate the resolved projection aspect to these consumers too. 2. src/core/rendering/upscaling.ts:74-82 only warms the new blit pipeline if a rectangle already exists (or another existing warm condition holds). For a fixed numeric pixelRatio and one initial full-target camera, adding CameraViewport after the first frame causes encodeUpscaling:100 to create its render pipeline during draw, violating the initialization/warm-up contract and causing a first-use hitch/pipeline warning. Warm this now-public dynamic path before the drain; add a late-rectangle test with numeric pixelRatio and no initial stack. Existing new tests use the default auto policy, which always warms it.
